WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.000
Visual regression testing with AI.

2
00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:06.134
Comparing two screenshots sounds foolproof.

3
00:00:06.134 --> 00:00:09.033
In practice it drowns teams in false alarms.

4
00:00:10.233 --> 00:00:12.100
Why is pixel diffing so noisy?

5
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:15.533
Anti aliasing changes how edges are smoothed.

6
00:00:15.833 --> 00:00:17.533
Font hinting nudges letters.

7
00:00:17.833 --> 00:00:20.167
A live timestamp changes every run.

8
00:00:20.467 --> 00:00:24.767
And a single pixel of sub pixel rendering is enough to trigger a failure.

9
00:00:25.967 --> 00:00:30.333
A perceptual or AI diff classifies changes the way a person would.

10
00:00:30.633 --> 00:00:31.943
That's just the clock.

11
00:00:31.943 --> 00:00:33.133
Mask it and move on.

12
00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:36.033
That's a layout shift, which is a real problem.

13
00:00:36.333 --> 00:00:37.979
And that's a colour change.

14
00:00:37.979 --> 00:00:42.367
Changes get grouped by kind, instead of every moved pixel being flagged.

15
00:00:43.567 --> 00:00:45.000
Here is the comparison.

16
00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:48.800
Pixel diff is fast and exact, but it flags everything.

17
00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:55.667
Perceptual diff is tolerant, and lets you ignore dynamic regions like clocks, ads, or live counters.

18
00:00:55.967 --> 00:00:57.967
It also groups changes by kind.

19
00:00:58.267 --> 00:01:03.667
Add a DOM structure diff too, to catch changes in an element's role or hierarchy.

20
00:01:04.867 --> 00:01:06.367
Here is how it goes wrong.

21
00:01:06.667 --> 00:01:12.626
The footer clock made naive pixel tests fail every single run, so the team disabled them.

22
00:01:12.626 --> 00:01:14.233
They lost real coverage.

23
00:01:15.433 --> 00:01:19.333
Back to the dashboard footer, which shows last updated with a live time.

24
00:01:19.633 --> 00:01:23.333
The team masks that one region and switches to perceptual diffing.

25
00:01:23.633 --> 00:01:25.500
False positives drop to zero.

26
00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:32.300
And next sprint it catches a real regression: a chart's y axis silently switching from linear to log.

27
00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:34.292
Let's recap.

28
00:01:34.292 --> 00:01:36.667
Pixel diffs produce false positives.

29
00:01:36.967 --> 00:01:39.733
Perceptual diffs classify changes by kind.

30
00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:41.633
Mask dynamic regions.

31
00:01:41.933 --> 00:01:44.353
And add a DOM structure check.

32
00:01:44.353 --> 00:01:45.967
Next up: agentic QA.
